#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063968 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0d0fdb7-9849-5aca-b494-3b013b2eb035" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Square Root of a Perfect Square Containing a Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "62a47fad-0c27-5e6d-a21f-8b298ac11159" :question/math-academy-id "e-1355" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4x^2}{y^2}}$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $4, x^2$, and $y^2$ are all perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{\\frac{4x^{2}}{y^{2}}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{2^{2}x^{2}}{y^{2}}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot x \\cdot x}{y \\cdot y}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\cdot x \\cdot 2 \\cdot x}{y \\cdot y}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{2x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{2x}{y}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{2x}{y})^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{(\\frac{2x}{y})^{2}} &= \\mid \\frac{2x}{y} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}2x &}{\\mid y\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & \\cdot & x &}{\\mid y\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\begin{vmatrix}x &}{\\mid y\\end{vmatrix}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064122 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bb1eda0-9577-59e7-9c6c-b85966fbb7e4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Square Root of a Perfect Square Using Absolute Value"}] :question/id #uuid "d71c8752-131c-5c82-b642-dee1d03484f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6737" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{16y^2}$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $16$ and $y^2$ are both perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{16y^{2}} &= \\sqrt{4^{2} \\cdot y^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 4 \\cdot y \\cdot y} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 \\cdot y \\cdot 4 \\cdot y} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4y \\cdot 4y} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(4y)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{(4y)^{2}} &= \\mid 4y \\mid \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & \\cdot & y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 4 \\mid y \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064141 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a48a060-871e-5c31-b1c0-7c4ed10eb259" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying the Square Root of a Perfect Square With Multiple Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "6bf774f8-aaa7-5a8e-9fb5-1ed1356b87ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-9639" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{36a^2c^2}$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $36, a^2$, and $c^2$ are perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{36a^{2}c^{2}} &= \\sqrt{6^{2} \\cdot a^{2} \\cdot c^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{6 \\cdot 6 \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot c \\cdot c} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{6 \\cdot a \\cdot c \\cdot 6 \\cdot a \\cdot c} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(6ac) \\cdot (6ac)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(6ac)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{(6ac)^{2}} &= \\mid 6ac \\mid \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}6 & \\cdot & ac\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6 \\mid ac \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0d0fdb7-9849-5aca-b494-3b013b2eb035" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Square Root of a Perfect Square Containing a Fraction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a78e917e-11b2-5db2-b8e2-1b4f31ad014e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± \\frac{p}{9q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061195} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0d9b8c-6095-5d0b-99f7-208fc4411b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{p}{9q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061196} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fa12f0-95d0-545a-abfd-d7bfe1cedcb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9 \\mid p \\mid}{q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061197} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e9dc1b-4efe-550c-bf27-9b453207bf43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9p}{\\mid q \\mid}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061198} {:answer/id #uuid "56d310f3-7461-58b5-a9b7-bc87ebafbbc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}p &}{9 \\mid q\\end{vmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061199}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fa61df20-116f-58fd-ad91-75f98fec1c35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061194}] :question/id #uuid "dae30c03-3248-5393-af33-6d1d5e3a7a98" :question/math-academy-id "q-129392" :question/problem "What is $\\sqrt{\\frac{p^{2}}{81q^{2}}}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0d0fdb7-9849-5aca-b494-3b013b2eb035" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Square Root of a Perfect Square Containing a Fraction"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "97082509-6da1-5d7c-a3a4-921738c6eb05" :question/math-academy-id "q-129471" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25{y}^{2}}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that ${z}^{2},25,$ and ${y}^{2}$ are all perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25{y}^{2}}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{5}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{z\\cdot z}{5\\cdot 5\\cdot y\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{z\\cdot z}{5\\cdot y\\cdot 5\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{z}{5y}\\cdot \\frac{z}{5y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{z}{5y})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{z}{5y})}^{2}} & =|\\frac{z}{5y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|z|}{|5y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|z|}{|5|\\cdot |y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|z|}{5|y|}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bb1eda0-9577-59e7-9c6c-b85966fbb7e4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Square Root of a Perfect Square Using Absolute Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87e6e7c0-5cc7-5cde-a201-3eefccfa4242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 3a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062258} {:answer/id #uuid "dccd486a-c092-5b76-91df-cb37bf8463ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3 \\mid a \\mid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062259} {:answer/id #uuid "e6059811-b154-518c-bedf-4cc3e1ea097f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062260} {:answer/id #uuid "e6fcf167-739d-5279-b53a-1bcbc9ec8c